> [After] the[ martyr]dom of St. Boniface, Vergilius was made[ Bishop] of Salzburg (766 or 767) and lab[oured] successfully for the upbuilding of his di[ocese] as well as for the spread of the Faith in neighbouring heathen countries, especially in Carinthia. He died at Salzburg, 27 November, 789. In 1233 he was canonized by[ Gregory] IX. His doctrine that the earth is a sphere was derived from the teaching of ancient ge[ographers], and his belief in the existence of the antipodes was probably influenced by the accounts which the ancient[ Irish] voyagers gave of their journeys. This, at least, is the opinion of Rettberg ("Kirc[heng]esch. Deutschlands", II,[ 236]).

